<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
      ⚠️ XML COMMENT RULE, AND IT COST A DAY: a double hyphen is ILLEGAL inside an
      XML comment. Writing a command like `script.sh - -flag` (without the space)
      anywhere in a comment in this file makes the whole answer file malformed.

      Windows Setup does not tell you. It parses Autounattend.xml, fails, and
      silently resets - so the VM boots WinPE, shows the Setup background with no
      UI drawn, writes nothing to the disk, reboots, and loops forever. It looks
      exactly like a driver or firmware problem and is neither.

      This file had `- -variant` (unspaced) in a comment. Symptom was a permanent
      boot loop; the whole answer file was being thrown away. Diagnosed by booting
      the same ISO on the same firmware with NO answer file, at which point Setup
      came up perfectly.

      If you edit comments here, validate afterwards:
        python3 -c "import xml.etree.ElementTree as E,re,sys;\
          E.fromstring(re.sub(r'\$\{[^}]+\}','X',open(sys.argv[1]).read()))" \
          packer/win11-client/cd/Autounattend.xml.pkrtpl
    -->
<!--
  packer/win11-client/cd/Autounattend.xml.pkrtpl

  WHAT THIS IS
    The Windows Setup answer file for golden template 9003 (tpl-win11-client),
    rendered by Packer's templatefile() and written to the root of a small generated
    ISO labelled PACKERCD.

  HOW IT DIFFERS FROM THE SERVER 2022 ANSWER FILE
    1. A RunSynchronous block writes the HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig bypass values.
       See the long comment on that block - the vTPM is real, so the interesting
       bypasses are the RAM and CPU ones.
    2. A specialize-pass BypassNRO value keeps OOBE local on the client SKU.
    3. Everything else is the same by design - including driver injection, which does
       NOT happen in this answer file: like the server, drivers arrive via the
       $WinPEDriver$ volume (the `w11` variant). Two answer files that diverge for no
       reason are two answer files nobody can diff.

  WHY IT EXISTS
    Windows Setup is an interactive installer. Every question it would ask a human -
    which disk, which edition, which keyboard, what password - has to be answered
    here or the build sits at a dialog until Packer's winrm_timeout expires.

  HOW WINDOWS FINDS IT
    Setup scans the root of every removable, read-only volume for a file named
    exactly "Autounattend.xml". No floppy drive is involved; the proxmox-iso builder
    does not support floppies at all.

  THE FOUR PASSES USED BELOW, IN THE ORDER WINDOWS RUNS THEM
    windowsPE   - runs inside the boot environment, before anything is on disk.
                  This is the ONLY place VirtIO drivers can be injected early enough
                  to matter, and where the disk gets partitioned.
    specialize  - runs after the image is applied, before first boot completes.
                  Machine identity: computer name, time zone, RDP.
    oobeSystem  - runs at the out-of-box experience. Local Administrator password,
                  autologon, and the FirstLogonCommands that bootstrap WinRM so
                  Packer can finally connect.

  ⚠️ THE PASSWORD BELOW IS IN PLAINTEXT.
    That is how Windows answer files work; there is no way around it for a password
    Setup itself must consume. It is a build-only credential supplied from the
    environment, the answer file's ISO is unmounted before the template is created,
    C:\Windows\Panther is scrubbed in 90-cleanup.ps1, and sysprep /generalize clears
    the LSA secret. Rotate the real Administrator password from Ansible anyway.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend"
          xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State"
          xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- PASS 1: windowsPE                                                     -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <settings pass="windowsPE">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <SetupUILanguage>
        <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      </SetupUILanguage>
      <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <!--
      THE POINT OF THIS WHOLE TEMPLATE.

      Windows Setup draws its disk list from whatever storage controllers WinPE can
      already see. WinPE ships no VirtIO driver, so on a virtio-scsi controller it
      shows an empty list and the install dead-ends. The historical workaround, and
      what every hand-built Windows VM in the old lab did, was to fall back to a SATA
      disk and an E1000 NIC - emulated 1990s hardware carried forward forever.
    -->
    <!--
      NO DRIVER INJECTION IN THIS ANSWER FILE AT ALL. Both answer-file mechanisms were
      tried on the server media (full history in win-server-2022/win-server.pkr.hcl)
      and neither works:

        PnpCustomizationsWinPE  -> "Windows could not apply the Windows PE bootstrap
                                    setting specified in the unattend answer file",
                                    even with every path verified to exist.
        RunSynchronous drvload  -> Setup reaches the disk list and still refuses the
                                    disk: drvload loads a driver into memory but does
                                    not STAGE it for injection into the installed OS,
                                    and a boot-critical controller that is not staged
                                    is rejected.

      The drivers now arrive via $WinPEDriver$ instead - a folder at the ROOT of an
      attached volume, which Windows Setup itself scans and drvloads (and stages for
      injection) before it draws the disk list. No answer-file involvement, so nothing
      here can reject it. That volume is built on the Proxmox host by
      build-winpe-driver-iso.sh (with the 'w11' variant flag) from the `w11` driver directories (not
      `2k22`), and attached as an extra CD (sata3) by this template.
    -->

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">

      <!--
        THE WINDOWS 11 HARDWARE-REQUIREMENT BYPASS.

        Windows 11 Setup refuses to install on a machine that fails its hardware
        checks, and the error it shows ("This PC can't run Windows 11") does not say
        WHICH check failed. These registry values, written into the WinPE registry
        before the check runs, turn the individual checks off.

        BE CLEAR ABOUT WHY EACH ONE IS HERE, BECAUSE IT IS NOT WHAT PEOPLE ASSUME:

          BypassTPMCheck        - BELT AND BRACES ONLY. Proxmox gives this VM a REAL
                                  vTPM 2.0 through tpm_config, and the OpenTofu module
                                  gives the cloned VM one too. The TPM check should
                                  pass on its own merits. This is here so that a
                                  mistyped tpm_config produces a working build with a
                                  missing device rather than an opaque Setup failure
                                  at minute 40 of a 90-minute build.
          BypassSecureBootCheck - same reasoning: OVMF with pre-enrolled keys satisfies
                                  this, but the certificate situation is in flux (the
                                  2011 Microsoft certificate set began expiring in
                                  June 2026).
          BypassRAMCheck        - THIS ONE IS LOAD-BEARING. Not because 4 GB fails the
                                  4 GB minimum, but because the check is fussy about
                                  how memory is reported, and this build sits exactly
                                  on the boundary.
          BypassCPUCheck        - ALSO LOAD-BEARING. cpu_type = "host" passes the
                                  physical CPU through, and Windows 11's supported-CPU
                                  list is a model allow-list, not a feature test. A
                                  perfectly capable host CPU that is not on Microsoft's
                                  list fails here.
          BypassStorageCheck    - the 64 GB minimum against a 60 GB disk. Sizing the
                                  disk to the licence check rather than to the lab's
                                  needs would be the tail wagging the dog.

        This is a lab workstation for detection engineering, not a machine Microsoft
        supports. Bypassing the checks is the honest configuration; pretending the
        hardware is something it is not would be worse.
      -->
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Create the LabConfig key</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Description>Bypass the TPM check (belt and braces - a real vTPM is present)</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassTPMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>3</Order>
          <Description>Bypass the Secure Boot check</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassSecureBootCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>4</Order>
          <Description>Bypass the RAM check</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassRAMCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>5</Order>
          <Description>Bypass the CPU model allow-list check</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassCPUCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>6</Order>
          <Description>Bypass the storage size check</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\System\Setup\LabConfig /v BypassStorageCheck /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>

      <!--
        GPT layout, because this VM boots OVMF/UEFI.

        An MBR DiskConfiguration will not boot under OVMF. The required shape is:
          1. EFI System Partition, FAT32, 260 MB  (the firmware reads this)
          2. Microsoft Reserved (MSR), 16 MB      (no filesystem, no letter)
          3. Windows, NTFS, everything else       (InstallTo points here)

        Note InstallTo PartitionID = 3, not 1. Getting that wrong installs Windows
        onto the 260 MB EFI partition and fails in a confusing way.

        WillWipeDisk = true is safe here and only here: this is a fresh Packer VM
        with one empty disk.
      -->
      <DiskConfiguration>
        <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        <Disk wcm:action="add">
          <DiskID>0</DiskID>
          <WillWipeDisk>true</WillWipeDisk>
          <CreatePartitions>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <Type>EFI</Type>
              <Size>260</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <Type>MSR</Type>
              <Size>16</Size>
            </CreatePartition>
            <CreatePartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <Type>Primary</Type>
              <Extend>true</Extend>
            </CreatePartition>
          </CreatePartitions>
          <ModifyPartitions>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>1</Order>
              <PartitionID>1</PartitionID>
              <Label>System</Label>
              <Format>FAT32</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>2</Order>
              <PartitionID>2</PartitionID>
            </ModifyPartition>
            <ModifyPartition wcm:action="add">
              <Order>3</Order>
              <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
              <Label>Windows</Label>
              <Letter>C</Letter>
              <Format>NTFS</Format>
            </ModifyPartition>
          </ModifyPartitions>
        </Disk>
      </DiskConfiguration>

      <ImageInstall>
        <OSImage>
          <!--
            Which edition to install. See the windows_image_index variable for how to
            confirm the index on YOUR ISO:
              dism /Get-WimInfo /WimFile:D:\sources\install.wim
          -->
          <InstallFrom>
            <MetaData wcm:action="add">
              <Key>/IMAGE/INDEX</Key>
              <Value>${image_index}</Value>
            </MetaData>
          </InstallFrom>
          <InstallTo>
            <DiskID>0</DiskID>
            <PartitionID>3</PartitionID>
          </InstallTo>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </OSImage>
      </ImageInstall>

      <UserData>
        <AcceptEula>true</AcceptEula>
        <FullName>MutaSpace SOC Lab</FullName>
        <Organization>MutaSpace</Organization>
%{ if product_key != "" ~}
        <ProductKey>
          <Key>${product_key}</Key>
          <WillShowUI>OnError</WillShowUI>
        </ProductKey>
%{ else ~}
        <!--
          No ProductKey element at all. Evaluation media carries its own edition and
          rejects a key. If you are installing from Volume Licensing media instead,
          set the product_key variable to the appropriate GVLK.
        -->
%{ endif ~}
      </UserData>

      <EnableFirewall>true</EnableFirewall>
      <EnableNetwork>true</EnableNetwork>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- PASS 2: specialize                                                    -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <settings pass="specialize">

    <!--
      PREVENT AUTOMATIC BITLOCKER DEVICE ENCRYPTION. Do not remove this.

      Windows 11 turns device encryption ON BY ITSELF during OOBE on a clean install
      when the firmware and a TPM support it - which describes this template exactly
      (q35 + OVMF + vTPM 2.0, all required for Win11). Nothing in the answer file asks
      for it.

      Sysprep /generalize CANNOT run on a BitLocker-encrypted volume. What that looks
      like from outside: the install completes, WinRM connects, every provisioner runs,
      then 99-sysprep.ps1 sits for 20+ minutes producing no output, the disk goes
      static, the console stays black and the VM never shuts down - so Packer waits on
      a shutdown that is never coming. No error is logged anywhere.

      Diagnosed by mounting the disk: `losetup` with partition scanning showed the Windows partition
      as FSTYPE "BitLocker" and its first sector carries the -FVE-FS- Full Volume
      Encryption signature, while the plain-NTFS recovery partition beside it mounted
      fine.

      This is also why the vTPM looked guilty earlier and why removing tpm_config
      "changed nothing": the vTPM IS involved, but as the trigger for auto-encryption,
      not as a reset. Removing it alone still left the build broken for the other
      reasons that were live at the time.

      PreventDeviceEncryption must be set BEFORE OOBE, which is why it lives in
      specialize rather than in a provisioner - by the time a provisioner could run,
      encryption has already begun and would have to be reversed with `manage-bde` in its off mode
      plus a wait for full decryption.

      BOTH specialize-pass commands live in this ONE component, and they must stay that
      way. A settings pass may name a component only once: a second
      Microsoft-Windows-Deployment block in the same pass makes the whole answer file
      invalid, and Setup fails with "The computer restarted unexpectedly or encountered
      an unexpected error" long before it reaches anything the file asks for. That is
      exactly what happened when PreventDeviceEncryption was first added as its own
      component - Windows named the fault precisely in Panther\setuperr.log ("The same
      namespace should not appear twice in a single settings section",
      hrResult = 0x8022001b), but nothing on the console or in the Packer log did.
      validate-answer-files.sh now checks for this.

      Command 2 (BypassNRO): Windows 11 22H2 and later push hard toward a Microsoft
      account during OOBE, and 24H2 removed the oobe\BypassNRO.cmd script people used to
      rely on. The unattend settings in the oobeSystem pass below (a local Administrator
      password plus AutoLogon) are what actually keep OOBE local; this registry value is
      the belt-and-braces version of the same thing, written before OOBE runs.
    -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Deployment"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <RunSynchronous>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Path>reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\BitLocker" /v PreventDeviceEncryption /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
          <Description>Prevent automatic BitLocker device encryption (blocks sysprep)</Description>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
        <RunSynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>2</Order>
          <Description>Allow OOBE to complete without a network or a Microsoft account</Description>
          <Path>reg.exe add HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\OOBE /v BypassNRO /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f</Path>
        </RunSynchronousCommand>
      </RunSynchronous>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <!--
        A build-time placeholder name, NOT win-client-01.

        This is a generalised template, and it is cloned more than any other machine in
        the lab - once for win-client-01 and again for every learner endpoint. The real
        computer name is set per-VM after cloning by Cloudbase-Init from the OpenTofu
        initialization block, and then confirmed when microsoft.ad.membership renames
        and domain-joins the machine in a single task.

        Baking a real name in here would give every learner the same computer name,
        which breaks domain join, breaks Group Policy, and - the reason it matters for
        this lab specifically - collapses every learner's telemetry into one Wazuh
        agent name, so the incident scenarios in docs/incident-scenarios/ can no longer
        tell whose workstation an event came from.
      -->
      <ComputerName>${computer_name}</ComputerName>
      <TimeZone>UTC</TimeZone>
    </component>

    <!--
      RDP on. This is a lab whose whole purpose is that learners look at things, and
      the Windows console through the Proxmox noVNC viewer is painful. UserAuthentication
      0 disables Network Level Authentication, which is a real weakening - it is
      accepted here because the machine sits behind fw-01 on an isolated lab LAN, and
      because turning NLA back on is itself a worthwhile hardening exercise.
    -->
    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-TerminalServices-LocalSessionManager"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <fDenyTSConnections>false</fDenyTSConnections>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-TerminalServices-RDP-WinStationExtensions"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <UserAuthentication>0</UserAuthentication>
    </component>
  </settings>

  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <!-- PASS 3: oobeSystem                                                    -->
  <!-- ==================================================================== -->
  <settings pass="oobeSystem">

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">
      <InputLocale>0409:00000409</InputLocale>
      <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
      <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
      <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
    </component>

    <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Shell-Setup"
               processorArchitecture="amd64"
               publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35"
               language="neutral"
               versionScope="nonSxS">

      <!--
        Every OOBE screen a human would click through.

        ProtectYourPC = 3 declines the optional telemetry/privacy defaults rather than
        accepting them. SkipMachineOOBE and SkipUserOOBE are deliberately NOT used:
        they have been deprecated for years and cause more breakage on current builds
        than the explicit Hide* settings they were meant to replace.
      -->
      <OOBE>
        <HideEULAPage>true</HideEULAPage>
        <HideLocalAccountScreen>true</HideLocalAccountScreen>
        <HideOEMRegistrationScreen>true</HideOEMRegistrationScreen>
        <HideOnlineAccountScreens>true</HideOnlineAccountScreens>
        <HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>true</HideWirelessSetupInOOBE>
        <NetworkLocation>Work</NetworkLocation>
        <ProtectYourPC>3</ProtectYourPC>
      </OOBE>

      <!--
        Setting AdministratorPassword enables the built-in Administrator account and
        is what stops OOBE from demanding that a new user be created.
      -->
      <!--
        THE `packer` ACCOUNT IS WHAT PACKER AUTHENTICATES AS. Do not remove it, and
        do not point winrm_username back at Administrator.

        Client Windows disables the BUILT-IN Administrator (RID 500) roughly five to
        ten minutes after Setup's final boot, on its own schedule, and after that no
        new WinRM shell can be created:

          Couldn't create shell: http response error: 401 - invalid content type

        Fourteen builds died on that. Measured across several of them:

          build 14:  boot 21:28:46 -> disabled 21:34:13   (boot +  5m27s)
          build 13:  boot 21:01:55 -> disabled 21:12:20   (boot + 10m25s)
          build 12:  boot 20:41:34 -> disabled 20:46:18   (boot +  4m44s)

        Five theories were tested against the running guest and every one was
        disproved - the AutoLogon LogonCount, 90-cleanup deleting the autologon
        registry values, the extra local admin the Cloudbase-Init MSI used to
        create, Defender quarantining the watchdog, and simply doing less work
        inside the window. See scripts/05-keep-admin-enabled.ps1 for the details.

        This sidesteps it instead. Windows only auto-disables the built-in account;
        a normal local administrator is left alone. `packer` is deleted again at the
        top of 99-sysprep.ps1, before the image is sealed, so it does not ship in
        the template or in any learner clone.

        AdministratorPassword still sets the built-in account's password, because
        that is what enables the account for the OOBE autologon and stops OOBE
        demanding that a new user be created. It is not how WinRM gets turned on any
        more - that is SetupComplete.cmd, staged in the specialize pass above.
      -->
      <UserAccounts>
        <!--
          ⚠️ THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO <AdministratorPassword> HERE. Do not add one.

          Every maintained public Windows-CLIENT Packer template picks exactly one of
          two account models, and never both:

            A. the built-in Administrator, with NO <LocalAccounts> at all;
            B. a dedicated local admin, with NO <AdministratorPassword> at all.

          This file used to do BOTH, which no working reference template does. Setting
          AdministratorPassword enables the built-in account for OOBE; client Windows
          then disables it again at the end of OOBE - that is documented, by design,
          and is the `Accounts: Administrator account status` policy whose effective
          default is Disabled on client and Enabled on Server. (Which is exactly why
          win-server-2022 builds fine with the identical arrangement.)

            https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-hardware/manufacture/desktop/
              enable-and-disable-the-built-in-administrator-account

          We are Camp B. The build authenticates as `packer`, autologon runs as
          `packer`, and 99-sysprep.ps1 deletes the account before the image is sealed.
          The nearest published analogue to this build - Pumba98/proxmox-packer-
          templates, proxmox-iso + Windows 11 Enterprise EVALUATION media + WinRM -
          has exactly this shape.
        -->
        <LocalAccounts>
          <LocalAccount wcm:action="add">
            <Name>packer</Name>
            <DisplayName>Packer build account</DisplayName>
            <Description>Temporary build account. Deleted by 99-sysprep.ps1.</Description>
            <Group>Administrators</Group>
            <Password>
              <Value>${admin_password}</Value>
              <PlainText>true</PlainText>
            </Password>
          </LocalAccount>
        </LocalAccounts>
      </UserAccounts>

      <!--
        Autologon exists to get OOBE to complete without a human at the console. It
        used to matter more than that: FirstLogonCommands do not run until somebody
        logs on, and they used to carry the WinRM bootstrap. That bootstrap now runs
        from SetupComplete.cmd instead - see the specialize pass - because running it
        at first logon meant Packer connected DURING OOBE and got cut off by
        Windows' end-of-OOBE housekeeping a few minutes later.

        ⚠️ CORRECTION: an earlier version of this comment claimed that reaching
        LogonCount 0 is what disables the built-in Administrator. THAT IS WRONG, and it
        sent the investigation down a blind alley. Microsoft's AutoLogon reference says
        the opposite - "If the built-in administrator account is used, the account is
        still active" after the count expires - and raising it 2 -> 5 changed nothing.

        What actually disables the built-in Administrator on client Windows is the end
        of OOBE, unconditionally. See the note on UserAccounts above. It no longer
        matters here, because the build no longer uses that account for anything. Every WinRM shell
        opened afterwards is then rejected, and Packer fails partway through the
        provisioners with:

          Couldn't create shell: http response error: 401 - invalid content type

        The build spends both of its logons (OOBE, then the windows-restart after
        10-cloudbase-init), so a count of 2 left it disabling the account mid-build,
        roughly four minutes into the second session. Confirmed on the box: Security
        event 4725 "A user account was disabled", subject and target both the built-in
        Administrator, immediately followed by StartMenuExperienceHost crash-looping.

        It presents as INTERMITTENT, because it is a race against however long
        90-cleanup's Optimize-Volume takes - one build got 99-sysprep uploaded before
        the account went away and three did not.

        This is also why win-server-2022 never hit it with the identical setting: on
        Server the built-in Administrator is the primary account and is not
        auto-disabled. Do not conclude from "Server works" that the setting is fine.

        5 leaves headroom for a driver install forcing an extra reboot while still
        never reaching 0 in a build that uses two. The AutoAdminLogon and
        DefaultPassword registry values this creates are deleted in
        scripts/90-cleanup.ps1 - note that the commonly-cited "AutoLogonCount = 0"
        trick is a logon-counter fix and does NOT scrub those values.
      -->
      <AutoLogon>
        <Enabled>true</Enabled>
        <Username>packer</Username>
        <LogonCount>5</LogonCount>
        <Password>
          <Value>${admin_password}</Value>
          <PlainText>true</PlainText>
        </Password>
      </AutoLogon>

      <TimeZone>UTC</TimeZone>

      <!--
        Find setup.ps1 by VOLUME LABEL, never by drive letter.

        Three CDs are attached during the build. Adding, removing or reordering one
        shifts every letter after it. The label is stable; D:/E:/F: are not.

        The command deliberately contains no & < or > characters - those are XML
        metacharacters and would have to be entity-escaped, which is a classic source
        of answer files that parse but do nothing. Dot-sourcing runs the script in the
        current session without needing the call operator.
      -->
      <!--
        WinRM comes up from here, at first logon. This is the arrangement that has
        actually worked: every build that reached WinRM did so through this command.

        ⚠️ IT RUNS DURING OOBE, and that has consequences - see the OOBE-completion
        section in docs/iac/resume-here.md. Moving it to SetupComplete.cmd was tried
        (87c0061, 58763a7) to avoid them and made things strictly worse: the
        specialize command that staged the .cmd aborted Windows Setup outright, so
        WinRM never appeared at all and two builds were lost. Do not retry that
        without reading the write-up first.

        The command deliberately contains no & < or > characters - those are XML
        metacharacters and would have to be entity-escaped, which is a classic source
        of answer files that parse but do nothing. Dot-sourcing runs the script in the
        current session without needing the call operator.
      -->
      <FirstLogonCommands>
        <SynchronousCommand wcm:action="add">
          <Order>1</Order>
          <Description>Bootstrap WinRM and the QEMU guest agent for Packer</Description>
          <RequiresUserInput>false</RequiresUserInput>
          <CommandLine>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "$v = Get-Volume | Where-Object { $_.FileSystemLabel -eq '${seed_cd_label}' } | Select-Object -First 1; . ($v.DriveLetter + ':\setup.ps1')"</CommandLine>
        </SynchronousCommand>
      </FirstLogonCommands>
    </component>
  </settings>
</unattend>
